WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:04.290
- Aw, are you setting up a lemonade stand?

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.123
- Yep.

00:00:05.123 --> 00:00:07.410
I've done market research,
set my prices accordingly,

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:10.110
and hired my first employee.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.420
She's only four, and her
resume was written in crayon

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.520
but she shows some serious potential.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:15.353
- Oh, really?

00:00:15.353 --> 00:00:17.580
Well, she's not exactly
known for follow through,

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.350
so how do you plan on keeping her around

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:20.820
when it gets really hot outside?

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.020
- I already thought about that.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.310
I'm gonna give her equity

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.920
alongside her 10 cents an hour pay.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.740
So the harder she works, the
higher the value of the company

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.630
and the more she'll ultimately make.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.630
- I sort of feel like lollipops
would be more effective.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.460
- But the competition is stiff out there.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.200
Places like Starbucks and Whole Foods

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.540
are offering this stuff
now and I gotta keep up.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:40.373
- Okay, hon.

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:41.940
Well, when she starts crying

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.100
about how complicated her taxes are,

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.724
don't say I didn't warn you.

00:00:45.724 --> 00:00:48.307
(bright music)

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.450
- When an employee is granted
ownership in the company

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.040
alongside a normal salary,

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.010
it's known as equity compensation.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:02.910
In the olden days,

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.260
this sort of thing was reserved

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.600
for highfalutin C-suite executives,

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.300
but due to changes in tax laws over time

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.900
they've enjoyed phases of
popularity since the 1950s.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.220
During the dot-com boom,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.870
tech companies left and right

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.750
used it as a way to
attract and retain talent.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:21.930
And even now, Silicon
Valley is seemingly crawling

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:23.550
with overnight millionaires,

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.470
all thanks to equity.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.140
Now it seems like everyone
from fast food joints

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.860
to grocery stores are offering
employees a way into the game.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.140
- Equity compensation is
great for the employer

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.400
for many reasons.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.650
It requires less cash flow on their part

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.300
and it encourages employees

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.760
to feel some investment in the company,

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.530
which can improve productivity

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.780
and keep employees around longer.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.330
They don't call 'em golden
handcuffs for nothing.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:50.940
- Of course, it's not just
good for the employers,

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.040
depending on when it's granted to you

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.450
and how much you profit,

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.990
it's possible you can pay less
tax on this form of income.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:59.790
If you join a company early on,

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:03.120
the shares you're granted
could also skyrocket in value.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:04.410
But this type of compensation

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.630
can come in a bunch of different forms,

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.190
which can be hard to understand,

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.370
and may end up feeling more
like a burden than a boon.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.340
So let's see if we can shed a
bit of light on these things.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.000
- Let's start with the most
well-known form: stock options.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.580
We've highlighted what these
are in a previous episode

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.990
but as refresher, a stock option
is the right to buy a stock

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:26.430
at a certain price.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:27.690
Why would this be valuable?

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.320
Well, if the stock goes up
in value, you can buy it,

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.920
also known as exercising
at the strike price,

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.050
the amount you have the
right to buy it for,

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.000
and then sell it at a profit.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.800
Usually there's a limited amount of time

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.450
in which you can take advantage of this,

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.400
generally around 10 years.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.560
- There are a few
different kinds of options,

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.420
but the most widely used are known

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.940
as non-qualified stock options.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.060
They can be granted to
anyone, management, employees,

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.010
even contractors and consultants,

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.320
but if you manage to
sell them for a profit,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.110
it's going to be be
taxed as ordinary income.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.090
The profits are not qualified

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:05.790
for beneficial tax treatment by the IRS.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:09.330
- Employee Stock Options
or ESOs work similarly

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.820
except they can only
be offered to employees

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.580
and there is usually a
vesting schedule attached.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:18.450
So for example, you might be
granted a thousand options,

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:21.300
but you only get access to 25% of them

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.950
after your first year,

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:26.670
then another 25% after
your second year and so on.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.500
Again, the purpose of this structure

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.750
is to keep you around for longer.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.720
Employee turnover is vastly expensive

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.690
so companies do everything
they can to avoid it.

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:39.180
- ISOs or incentive stock options

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.950
are usually granted by companies

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:42.960
that are already publicly traded

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.330
or intent on becoming so shortly.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.330
They're offered to key
employees in top management.

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:51.510
But why would someone that's
able to command a high salary

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.150
be interested in this
form of compensation?

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.730
It basically comes down to tax savings.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:59.940
- Take Roberta, she's a highly
qualified software engineer

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:01.980
and has two different job offers.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:05.940
One from an established company
at a 200 grand base salary

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:07.470
and the other one, from a startup

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:11.910
offering her 160 grand with
a grant of ISOs upon signing

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:14.490
that are currently worth about 40K.

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:16.110
If she takes option one,

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:19.200
that would put her in the 32% tax bracket.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.630
Now, that doesn't mean she's paying 32%

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:23.550
of every dollar she's making,

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:24.383
for more on that,

00:04:24.383 --> 00:04:27.420
check out our video on how
progressive taxation works,

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:28.830
but it's significant.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.750
If she takes the second option,

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:33.270
she stays in the 24% bracket

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:37.380
and she'll only pay 15%
capital gains on the options.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:38.910
Sounds good, right?

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:40.860
- If only it were so straightforward.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:44.340
Something you have to watch
out for is the bargain element.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:45.540
This is the difference

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.150
between the market price
and the strike price.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:51.030
Essentially, the discount
you get on the option.

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:54.480
If Roberta's company stock
is worth $100 per share

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.210
and she can buy it for as little as $10,

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:59.490
the bargain element is $90

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.890
and that's taxed as ordinary income.

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:04.050
If she held the stock
for a little bit longer

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:08.820
after she exercised it and
it goes up to $120 a share,

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:10.500
then the $20 is subject

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:13.560
to the more favorable
capital gains tax rate.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.470
- Her shares must also be
held for more than one year

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:17.910
from the date she sells them

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:20.190
and two years from the
time they were granted

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.840
for her to get that benefit.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:23.850
This could possibly keep her stuck

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:25.980
in a job she doesn't end up liking.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.920
And if the stock price goes down instead,

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.860
she could be underwater on her options.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:34.080
Executing a large amount of ISOs

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.120
could also potentially flip her

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.340
into an alternate tax universe

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:41.400
called the alternative minimum tax.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.080
So proceed with caution.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.870
- Another way that employers
offer access to equity

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:50.610
is through an employee
stock purchase plan or ESPP.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:53.700
It's similar to a 401k, in
that it involves setting up

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:55.170
a deduction from your paycheck,

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.330
which is used to purchase company stock

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:00.780
at a discounted price,
sometimes as much as 15%.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:02.130
Pretty sweet, right?

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:03.900
Of course, there are some catches.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.390
The IRS puts limits on
how much you can shovel

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:07.680
into such a thing,

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.380
and timing the sale can
leave you in the position

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.260
of having to choose between
diversifying your portfolio

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.060
or possibly getting a tax break

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.220
if you hold onto them an extra year.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:18.660
A tough call for many.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:21.780
- Of course, a company could
just give you stock outright.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:25.710
Employees are generally granted
stock in the form of RSUs

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:28.410
or restricted stock units.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:31.890
When they're initially granted,
they're yours only on paper.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.960
You can't sell them
off until they've vest,

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.450
which they do in chunks over time.

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:39.600
Once vested, they are
considered taxable income

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.780
for that year, even if you
don't sell them immediately.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:44.640
Thankfully, unlike options,

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:47.820
RSU's always have some value to you

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:51.390
even if they go down in value
from the time of the grant.

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:53.220
But remember, if you leave the company,

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:56.490
the unvested shares finish into the air.

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:58.260
- If you're offered a significant amount

00:06:58.260 --> 00:06:59.760
of equity compensation,

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:02.760
hiring a professional
planner who is a fiduciary

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:04.770
will enable you to make the best calls

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:06.630
for your overall financial health.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:09.150
But here are three main
things to look out for

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:10.950
when trying to make a decision.

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:14.370
- Number one, figure
out your tax liability.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:15.900
The form that this compensation

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.960
takes in the dates at which
you decide to sell things

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:22.500
can make a massive difference
on how much you pocket.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.470
So reach out to a CPA
if you have questions.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:27.480
Sometimes, even your HR department

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.060
will be able to provide
you with some guidance.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:33.210
- Number two, watch out
for diversification.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:36.180
When you're relying on a
single company's performance,

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:38.910
not only for your income
but also your net worth,

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:40.650
it can get really risky.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:43.080
Hopefully you're working for
a company that you believe in

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:45.450
but don't let that blind
you to the possibility

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:48.510
that things can take a
downturn at any time.

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:50.340
- Number three, remember,

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.590
this company is not
granting you this stuff

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:55.050
out of the kindness of
their corporate hearts.

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:57.090
They want to keep you from jumping ship

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:58.560
when the going gets tough.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.100
- But money is a renewable
resource, time is not.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:04.710
Don't let things like
possible future payouts

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:06.720
keep you from seeking out a healthier

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.210
or more rewarding workplace.

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:11.550
- [Both] And that's our two cents.

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:14.070
- Thanks to our patrons
for keeping "Two Cents"

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:15.660
financially healthy.

